==Home media==
In early 1980, Paramount Home Video issued a limited series of VHS tapes with two episodes per volume, pursuant to the theatrical release of ''[[Star Trek: The Motion Picture]]'', with a selling price of $79.99 per volume.

Beginning in March 1981, a handful of season-one episodes debuted on [[Capacitance Electronic Disc|CED]]. With the release of ''[[Star Trek II: The Wrath of Khan]]'' in theatres in June 1982, Paramount Home Video took a gamble on releasing the season-one episode "[[Space Seed]]" (along with a handful of theatrical titles) on public sale for a reduced price of $29.99, at a time when videotape titles typically sold for around $80.<ref name="startrek.com">{{cite web|url=https://intl.startrek.com/article/space-seed-khan-forever-changed-vhs-releases|title="Space Seed," Khan Forever Changed VHS Releases|date=February 1, 2018|access-date=August 15, 2021}}</ref> The move put the episode into the top sales position for the remainder of the year (helping to establish the “sell-thru” retail market), and into 1983, when ''Star Trek II'' went on public sale for $39.95. A complete set of season-one episodes debuted from 1985 through 1989 on [[laserdisc]], [[VHS]], and [[Betamax]] formats, typically with two episodes per volume (“The Menagerie”, Parts 1 and 2 appeared the year prior on laserdisc). [[Columbia House]] began issuing mail-order volumes featuring episodes in "star date" order in 1986.

The original series pilot, "The Cage", was released to home video in 1986, consisting of black-and-white workprint footage combined with color footage from the season-one, two-part episode [[The Menagerie (Star Trek: The Original Series)|"The Menagerie"]] with a run time of 73 minutes. In 1988, the excised color footage was found and the full-color version of the episode debuted on television and was later given a home-video release for the first time, with a shorter run time of 64 minutes.

Beginning in August 1999, season-one episodes debuted on DVD, with two episodes per volume, completing the entire series, including both versions of "The Cage", in November 1991. A complete-season box set with new bonus features, but without either version of the pilot, was released in August 2004.

Season one was released on the [[HD-DVD]] format as a 10-disc set in late 2007.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.engadget.com/2007-07-26-star-trek-the-original-series-hd-dvd-boxed-set-hits-november-20.html|title=Star Trek: The Original Series S1 HD DVD boxed set hits November 20|date=July 26, 2007|access-date=August 15, 2021}}</ref> The set included the series with remastered effects on hybrid DVD/HD-DVD combo discs, which play in DVD players at standard definition, but also on appropriate HD-DVD players.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trekmovie.com/2008/08/07/cbs-announces-trek-remastered-complete-series-dvd-set/|title=CBS Announces Trek Remastered Complete Series DVD Set|date=August 7, 2008|access-date=August 15, 2021}}</ref> HD-DVD was overall discontinued, so only season one was released on HD-DVD, although the later two seasons were still released as remastered DVD versions. By purchasing a HD-DVD player and a remastered HD-DVD ''Star Trek'' season one, buyers of this special promotion could acquire a remote control shaped like ''Star Trek'' original-series [[Weapons in Star Trek#Phaser|phaser]] prop.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://trekmovie.com/2007/09/21/more-details-on-toshiba-phaser-tos-r-promotion/|title=More Details On Toshiba Phaser TOS-R Promotion|date=September 21, 2007|access-date=August 15, 2021}}</ref> Toshiba had partnered with Paramount to release the original series in remastered format, to support its then-new HD-DVD optical video disc format.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.cdrinfo.com/d7/content/toshiba-and-cbs-release-star-trek-original-series-hd-dvddvd-combo-disc|title=Toshiba and CBS to Release "Star Trek: The original Series" in HD DVD/DVD Combo Disc|date=November 22, 2007|access-date=August 15, 2021}}</ref>

The  ''[[Star Trek: Beyond the Final Frontier]]'' documentary was included with the season-one HD DVD box set of ''Star Trek: The Original Series.''<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wright|first=Matt|title=Review – Star Trek: The Original Series Season One HD DVD Box Set [UPDATED]|url=https://trekmovie.com/2007/11/17/review-star-trek-the-original-series-season-one-hd-dvd-box-set/|access-date=March 18, 2021|website=TrekMovie.com|language=en-US}}</ref> However, the documentary was included in the DVD component of the set, not the HD portion.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Star Trek : The Original Series - Season 1 Digitally Remastered (DVD On One Side & HD On The Other) [HD DVD]- Pre-owned|url=https://hddvd-revived.com/index.php?route=product/product&product_id=576|access-date=March 18, 2021|website=hddvd-revived.com}}</ref>

The first season, with remastered effects, debuted on [[Blu-ray]] on April 28, 2009.  On September 6, 2016, it was re-released as part of the Star Trek 50th Anniversary set, which included the three seasons of the original series, original-cast films up to ''Star Trek VI'', and the 1970s animated series on Blu-Ray optical discs.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://cinapse.co/star-trek-50th-anniversary-tv-and-movie-collection-the-complete-adventures-of-kirk-co-ff5c5207909b|title=STAR TREK 50th Anniversary TV and Movie Collection: The Complete Adventures of Kirk & Co. [Blu-review]|date=October 14, 2016|access-date=August 15, 2021}}{{Dead link|date=September 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

==Reception==
In 2019, [[Comic Book Resources|CBR]] rated season one of ''Star Trek'' (1966–1969 series) as the 10th-best season of all ''Star Trek'' seasons up to that time, comparing it to seasons of later series.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cbr.com/every-star-trek-tv-season-ranked/|title=Every Star Trek Season of TV Ever, Ranked from Worst to Best|date=January 4, 2019|website=CBR|language=en-US|access-date=March 16, 2019}}</ref>

